<div class="card">


    <h1>
        About Me
    </h1>


    <p>
        Hello! I'm Sophie Schöneberg, a Data Science Master's
        student at the University of Mannheim with a strong focus
        on corporate security management and data science.
    </p>


    <h2>
        Background &amp; Focus
    </h2>


    <p>
        I completed my Bachelor's degree in Political Science at
        the University of Mannheim, where I focused on International
        Relations and Statistics with excellent grades. My work
        centered on large-scale data projects, such as analysing
        sentiment differences in independent and state-controlled
        news articles, where I examined over 20,000 articles and
        their commentary across various economic, social, and
        political events. I primarily work with R and Python to
        develop the projects available on my page. I wrote my
        Bachelor's Thesis on the growing impact of dual-use
        satellites in space and the dangers that come with
        blurring the lines between civil and military objects.
    </p>


    <p>
        I also work as a Student Researcher at the Chair of
        International Relations, where I collect information on
        pro-government militias, paramilitaries, and private
        security companies for various countries, with a primary
        focus on Africa and Asia. If you are interested in learning
        more about me, feel free to download my resume or message
        me on your preferred communication channel.
    </p>


    <h2>
        Interests
    </h2>


    <p>
        My interests lie in both qualitative and quantitative
        analyses of security-related events, specifically bridging
        the gap between political science and advanced data science.
        With my skill set, I aim to drive data-driven decision-making
        in the fields of Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI), Open Source
        Intelligence (OSINT), and Geopolitical Risk Modeling within
        corporate security and supply chain environments. Backed by
        my academic background and technical expertise, I am motivated
        by contemporary global disruptions and their impacts on
        critical infrastructure, logistics, and international business.
        Here are a few questions I find compelling:
    </p>


    <div class="questions">

        <p>
            <span class="question">01 //</span>
            How can we automatically detect and track supply chain
            vulnerabilities or maritime/aviation disruptions using
            open-source data and anomaly detection? When a single
            cargo ship blocks a global trade hub, what mathematical
            structure tells us in advance where the next fragile
            bottleneck in the global supply chain will break?
        </p>


        <p>
            <span class="question">02 //</span>
            Can Natural Language Processing (NLP) map and forecast
            geopolitical tensions and state-sponsored disinformation
            campaigns in real-time?
        </p>


        <p>
            <span class="question">03 //</span>
            How do we transition from reactive corporate security
            to predictive threat intelligence by fusing hard data
            streams with qualitative geopolitical analysis?
        </p>


        <p>
            <span class="question">04 //</span>
            How can we use nocturnal satellite imagery and light
            anomalies to predict actual economic activity or trouble
            spots in lockdown regions where all official statistics
            are manipulated?
        </p>

    </div>


</div>


